    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d242">

                <pb facs="0290" n="188" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg188"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/4927: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Consul General at Stockholm</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Halstead</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-05-15T18:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 15, 1919, 6
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Personal to be deciphered by Halstead.</p>
                <p>Secretary of State desires you proceed at once to Vienna via Paris as successor
                    to Coolidge, American Commissioner in Austria.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d242-f1"/>
                    How soon can you leave and telegraph whether Hopper<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d242-f2"/> is capable of administering Consulate General?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d242-f1" n="3" xml:id="d242-f1-content">
                <p>This title had not, in fact, been used for Professor Coolidge.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d242-f2" n="4" xml:id="d242-f2-content">
                <p>George Dunlop Hopper, Consul at Stockholm.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
